    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_bjm_111'), 'Pull request "enroll in the background to speed up checkout":

  @Post(":courseId/enroll")
- async enroll(...) {
-   await this.enrollments.payWithPointsAndEnroll(user.id, courseId);   // one transaction, p99 45 ms
-   return { enrolled: true };
- }
+ @HttpCode(HttpStatus.ACCEPTED)
+ async enroll(...) {
+   await this.enrollQueue.add("pay-and-enroll", { userId: user.id, courseId });
+   return { status: "queued" };
+ }

The web app navigates to /learn/<courseId> as soon as the request returns. That page calls GET /courses/<courseId>/lessons, which answers 403 when no enrollment exists. Queue dashboard: jobs wait 0.2-1.5 s before a worker picks them up.

Statement: "Because the job runs within a second or two, this change is safe: the learner can open the course right after the 202."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The very next request reads the enrollment: the browser opens /learn/<courseId> within milliseconds of the 202, while the job waits 0.2-1.5 s before it even starts, so the learner gets 403 on a course they just paid for, and under load the wait grows (lesson 10). The step is also fast (45 ms p99), local (one database transaction) and something the user must see succeed now, including the case where it fails because the balance is too low. That is exactly the kind of work that stays in the request. A background job fits work whose result the response does not need; here the response is the result. The misconception is that "fast enough in the background" equals "done": a job that usually runs soon is still not done when the response leaves.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_bjm_111') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
