WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_bjm_121'), 'The fix proposed in code review for the grading worker:

UPDATE submissions SET status = $2, score = $3
WHERE id = $1 AND status = ''running'';

(the worker sets status = ''running'' when it picks up the job). Job log of another submission after the fix:

10:03:00.351  grade:s_90120  stalled, lock expired, moved back to wait   (w-3 paused)
10:03:00.902  grade:s_90120  active worker=w-7, sets status=''running''
10:03:01.410  w-3 resumes, its sandbox run ended: failed (wall time exceeded)
10:03:01.415  w-3  UPDATE ... status=''failed'' WHERE id=''s_90120'' AND status=''running''
10:03:04.118  w-7  sandbox result: passed, runs the same UPDATE with ''passed''

Statement: "With this WHERE clause, w-3''s late ''failed'' at 10:03:01.415 updates zero rows, so the submission ends as passed."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. At 10:03:01.415 the status is ''running'' again, because w-7 set it when it picked up the job at 10:03:00.902. The condition status = ''running'' is true, so w-3''s stale ''failed'' is written (rowCount 1). Then w-7''s ''passed'' at 10:03:04.118 finds status = ''failed'' and updates zero rows: the submission ends as failed, the opposite of what the review intended. A state check rejects writes after a job has ended, but it cannot tell two attempts of the same running job apart. The update needs the owner as well, for example AND worker_id = $4 with an id unique per attempt (an attempt number or lock token), so only w-7 can finish attempt 2. The misconception is that "running" identifies one execution; it only identifies a state that several executions can pass through.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_bjm_121') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
