const won = await this.exports.succeed(exportId, workerId);
// UPDATE exports SET status = ''succeeded'', worker_id = NULL, finished_at = now()
// WHERE id = $1 AND status = ''running'' AND worker_id = $2      -> returns rowCount > 0
if (won) await this.mail.sendExportReady(exportId);
else this.logger.warn({ exportId, workerId }, "stale result discarded");

Log:
14:20:12  admin u_0007  POST /admin/exports/x_311/cancel -> 200 {"status":"cancelled"}
14:40:55  w-2  x_311 upload done (2.1 GB)
14:40:56  w-2  succeed x_311 rowCount=0
14:40:56  w-2  WARN stale result discarded

Statement: "rowCount=0 means the export was cancelled or handed to another worker after w-2 started it, and with this code the instructor does not get an ''export ready'' email."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The update only matches a row that is still running and still owned by w-2. Zero rows means one of those changed since w-2 claimed it: here the admin''s cancel at 14:20:12 moved it to cancelled (a lease timeout and a new owner would also give zero rows). Because the email is sent only when won is true, the side effect follows the transition instead of the work, so no link for a cancelled export goes out. Note what the fix does not do: w-2 still spent twenty minutes uploading 2.1 GB, because cancelling a transition does not stop a running worker; checking ownership in the heartbeat or between steps lets it stop early. The misconception is that the email belongs to the upload; it belongs to the successful transition.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_bjm_121') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
