    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_bjm_211'), 'After the incident a first fix was deployed:

async process(job: Job<{ userId: string; courseId: string }>) {
  return this.db.transaction(async (tx) => {
    const claimed = await tx.query(
      `INSERT INTO processed_jobs (key) VALUES ($1) ON CONFLICT (key) DO NOTHING RETURNING key`,
      [`job:${job.id}`],
    );
    if (claimed.length === 0) return "duplicate";
    // ... insert the certificate and the outbox event
  });
}

A day later the duplicate check finds another pair. API and worker logs:

09:41:03.100 api POST /enrollments/e_77/complete -> enqueue issue-certificate user=u_binh course=sql-basics: timeout after 2000 ms, retrying
09:41:05.180 api enqueue issue-certificate user=u_binh course=sql-basics -> job 9002
09:41:05.410 certificates-worker-1 job=9001 issued certificate C-6010 user=u_binh course=sql-basics
09:41:05.530 certificates-worker-4 job=9002 issued certificate C-6011 user=u_binh course=sql-basics

Statement: "Keying the processed-jobs table by the job id cannot catch this duplicate; a key built from the user id and the course id would."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The first enqueue call timed out on the API side but did reach the queue (job 9001), and the API''s retry created a second job (9002) for the same completion. Both are first deliveries with different ids, so a key made from job.id sees two new keys and lets both through. The business rule is one certificate per learner and course, so a key built from exactly those fields, certificate:u_binh:sql-basics, makes the second job a duplicate, whatever job carried it. A job-id key only catches redeliveries of the same message. The misconception is that the job id identifies the work; it identifies one message, and a producer that retries can create several messages for the same work.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_bjm_211') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
