Evidence C - queue and consumer config, as deployed:
queue: grade-submissions
type: fifo
messageGroupId: "grading"      # one group: results are published in submission order
consumer:
  concurrency: 1               # per message group
retry:
  attempts: 0                  # 0 = unlimited
  backoff: { type: fixed, delay: 30s }
  onFailure: requeue-head
deadLetterQueue: none
alerts: none on queue depth or message age

Statement: "sub_8812 fails, goes back to the head of the only message group, and with concurrency 1 and unlimited attempts nothing behind it can start, so the queue grows by the arrival rate."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. This hypothesis explains all of the evidence. C allows one in-flight message per group, puts every submission in one group, requeues a failure at the head and never gives up (attempts: 0). B shows the result: the same message every 30 s. A shows the consequence: waiting grows by about 445 per minute, exactly the arrival rate, and the oldest message age grows with the clock. One message that can never succeed (a poison message) plus unlimited retries plus strict ordering is head-of-line blocking. Remove any one of the three and the incident does not happen: the message would be parked, or would block only its own group, or would be retried a few times and given up.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_bjm_311') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
