WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_bjm_431'), 'Certificates worker:

process.on("SIGTERM", () => process.exit(0));

Deploy log and job state:
11:02:14  pod certificates-7f9 received SIGTERM (rolling deploy)
11:02:14  job cert-5521 in progress: PDF stored, email sent, enrollment not yet marked certificate_issued
11:02:14  process exited, cert-5521 not acknowledged
queue setting: visibility timeout 15 min

Statement: "Exiting at once is safe here: the broker redelivers cert-5521 and the learner ends up with exactly one certificate email, a few seconds later."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False, on both counts. The message becomes visible again only when its visibility timeout runs out, so cert-5521 is retried about 15 minutes later, not a few seconds later. And the email step already ran: when the job runs again from the start, the learner gets a second email unless the email step is idempotent (a sent marker or an idempotency key at the provider, lessons 4 and 9). A graceful shutdown stops taking new jobs on SIGTERM, lets in-flight jobs finish within a deadline shorter than the platform''s grace period, and releases any job it cannot finish (for SQS, ChangeMessageVisibility to 0) so it is redelivered at once. The misconception is that at-least-once delivery makes crashes free: it makes them recoverable, at the cost of a delay and a duplicate run that the job must be written to survive.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_bjm_431') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO challenge.question_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct)
SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', false),
        ('False', true)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);
WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_bjm_431'), 'Release v42 changes the certificate job payload. The API (producer) and the certificate workers ship in the same image, deployed pod by pod:

v41 payload  { "userId": "u_81", "courseId": "c_12" }
v42 payload  { "v": 2, "learnerId": "u_81", "courseId": "c_12" }

11:30:40  api pod-1 now v42, enqueues v2 payloads
11:31:05  worker pod-3 (v41)  cert-6001  Error: userId is required  attempt 1/5
11:33:12  worker pod-3 (v41)  cert-6001  Error: userId is required  attempt 5/5 -> DLQ
11:36:00  certificates DLQ: 214 messages, all v2 payloads handled by v41 workers

Statement: "Releasing workers that accept both payload shapes first, and switching the producer to v2 only after every worker runs that release, would have avoided these failures."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. During a rolling deploy old and new code run side by side, and jobs outlive the deploy that created them. If every worker can read v1 and v2 before any producer writes v2, no worker ever receives a payload it cannot read; v1 support is removed in a later release, once no v1 job is left in the queue, in delayed retries or in the DLQ. Here the producer and the consumer changed in the same release, so new payloads reached old workers. The 214 messages can be redriven from the DLQ after the fix, because a v42 worker reads them. The misconception is that a deploy is atomic: for queues, the payload is a contract between versions that are running at the same time, so it needs a version field and expand-then-contract changes.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_bjm_431') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
)
